  there's nothing to inject values at deploy time unless you add one.

  - [1mHostname detection[0m — settings module maps [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m / staging host / prod host to endpoints; deploy the same file
    everywhere, it self-selects. Costs: the host list lives in client-side code, and a new environment means a code
    change. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mSingle edited value[0m — settings module holds one [38;5;153mENVIRONMENT = "dev"[39m line you flip before deploying. Simplest
    possible; costs: a manual step that's easy to get wrong, and prod/staging artifacts differ by an edit rather than by
    the host.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — a build step substitutes the endpoint per environment. Most robust for real deployments;
    costs: introduces tooling (bundler or a script) to a repo that currently has none. Hardest to walk back.
  - [1mRuntime override[0m — query param or [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m beats a fixed choice; realistically a dev-convenience [3mlayer[0m on top of
    one of the above, not a standalone answer.

  [1mMy recommendation: hostname detection.[0m It matches what the repo actually is — a static page with no toolchain — and it
  makes environment switching automatic rather than a thing you remember to do. If a build step arrives later, the
  settings module is the natural place to read an injected value, so this doesn't box you in.

